  <graph edgedefault="undirected">
    <node id="&quot;JUSTICIA CALIFORNICA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SPECIES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Justicia californica is a species of flowering shrub native to the deserts of southern California, Arizona, and northern Mexico. Its common names include chuparosa (or chiparosa, both colloquial Spanish terms for "hummingbird"</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHUPAROSA&quot;">
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chuparosa is a colloquial Spanish term for 'hummingbird' and is one of the common names for Justicia californica, indicating a cultural association with hummingbirds."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BELOPERONE&quot;">
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a</data>
      <data key="d1">"Beloperone is another common name for Justicia californica, highlighting its multiple names and cultural significance."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GREVILLEA THELEMANNIANA&quot;">
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a</data>
      <data key="d1">"Spider-net grevillea is a common name for Grevillea thelemanniana, indicating its descriptive nature and usage in naming the species."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPIDER-NET GREVILLEA&quot;">
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a</data>
      <data key="d1">"Spider-net grevillea is a common name for Grevillea thelemanniana, indicating its descriptive nature and usage in naming the species."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HUMMINGBIRD BUSH&quot;">
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hummingbird bush is a common name for both Grevillea thelemanniana and Justicia californica, suggesting a shared characteristic or habitat with hummingbirds."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CENTROPOGON&quot;">
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a</data>
      <data key="d1">"The shapes of hummingbird beaks facilitate both nectar feeding and pollination of long tubular Centropogon flowers, indicating a coevolutionary relationship."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SICKLEBILL BEAK&quot;">
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a</data>
      <data key="d1">"The shapes of hummingbird beaks facilitate both nectar feeding and pollination of long tubular Centropogon flowers, indicating a coevolutionary relationship."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
